WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:03.003
My name is Noah Planavsky, and I'm a geochemist.

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:05.547
Where a lot of my research is focused right now

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:08.508
is trying to understand what controls carbon dioxide levels

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:14.472
and how we can change carbon dioxide levels over the next 100 years to have greater climate stability.

00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:18.768
I originally went to school as a geologist and I thought I was going to

00:00:18.768 --> 00:00:22.689
wander Earth's surface just making observations
in the rock record.

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:26.651
But I found that most of the questions
that I was interested in

00:00:26.651 --> 00:00:30.697
were better solved through geochemical tools
and through geochemical modeling

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.282
than field observations alone.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.455
The type of geochemistry that I do is understanding how individual reactions

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:43.877
occurring in organisms within soil or within
the ocean

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:52.218
will affect the atmosphere, will affect climate on a global scale.

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:54.763
For me, what's going to be the next step
in geochemistry

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:57.223
and what's going to make geochemistry more impactful

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:58.767
is the core of my research program,

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:01.811
which is a better integration of field observations,

00:01:01.811 --> 00:01:04.939
novel geochemical tools, and tying that into a robust

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:08.068
and meaningful quantitative framework.

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:10.945
Of course, it makes sense
to have all of those components combined.

00:01:10.945 --> 00:01:14.991
Having a research program
that does that well

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:20.455
and that ensures that all of those parts are greater
than what they would be individually

00:01:20.455 --> 00:01:22.749
is I think what's going to be

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:26.920
the important component of moving forward
our understanding over the next decade.

